{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-criteria-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the POLLUTANT dimension: the `expand-criteria` run -- THC, CO and NOx running exhaust -- computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. It is the seventh RunSpec this domain document has been asked for and the first to leave the energy and particulate pollutants, and it is PENDING.\n\nWHAT IT WAITS ON, which is two things and both are named rather than suspected.\n\nFIRST, THE OTHER HALF OF `criteriaRatio`. That table is EMPTY in the default database -- MOVES's `FuelEffectsGenerator` computes all 4,398 of its rows at run time -- and it computes them TWO WAYS, which is the whole reason this document is still here. For model years 2001 and later, and for diesel at every model year, MOVES evaluates a closed-form expression the database ships as SQL text in `generalFuelRatioExpression`. For gasoline model years 2000 and earlier it instead runs the EPA COMPLEX MODEL live, over `complexModelParameters`, `meanFuelParameters`, `fuelModelWtFactor` and `baseFuel`, and follows it with the sulfur model out of `sulfurModelCoeff`.\n\nThe first way is ported and measured: 309 of this run's 372 cells now land inside tolerance at a worst 7.804e-06, which is the residual every other RunSpec in this directory carries. The second is not, and the 63 cells it owns are exactly the gasoline cohorts of model years 1980 to 2000 on all three pollutants. They are HIGH -- by 1.2 % to 9.2 % on THC, 3.8 % to 15.7 % on CO and 3.5 % to 3.9 % on NOx -- and high by precisely the reduction the missing ratio would have applied: `criteriaRatio` is 0.9158 for gasoline THC before model year 1994 and 0.9880 from 1994 to 2000, and 1/0.9158 is 1.0919. PLAN.md §7.3.14 has the port and what is left of it.\n\nWHAT THE FIRST HALF COST AND WHAT IT BOUGHT. Five new data sources -- `regionCounty`, `FuelSupply`, `FuelFormulation`, `FuelSubtype` and `e10FuelProperties` -- and 135 rows of coefficients extracted from the 135 expression strings whose pollutant-process is 101, 201 or 301. The extraction is checked against an independent parse and evaluation of all 135 strings over all 2,158 formulations, 165,297 cells at a worst 1.4e-14, and then against this snapshot's own captured `criteriaRatio` for all 2,013 of its rows that the closed form covers, at a worst 4.4e-08 -- which is not a modelling residual but the binary32 question docs/fuel-effects-generator.md §7.2 decides, and is 450 times inside this repository's per-cell tolerance.\n\nONE THING THE FIRST HALF MADE VISIBLE that no other document in this repository could. The default database ships the E85 formulation 27002 with DISTILLATION SENTINELS -- `T50` 999, `T90` 999, `ETOHVolume` 74 -- and the THC criteria polynomial carries `5.58e-05*T50*T50`. Read straight, that returns a fuel-effect ratio of 1.0e+17. `FuelEffectsGenerator.setup()` step 020 overwrites those properties from `e10FuelProperties` before any fuel-effect arithmetic runs, and the snapshot corpus cannot show this at all: every captured `fuelformulation` table in it is the EXECUTION copy, with the substitution already applied. It is only visible from the default database, which is what this rung of PLAN.md exists to read.\n\nSECOND, the SURVIVOR COUNT is declared here and not recomputed, and the `n_outputCohort` binding below is what papers over it. The chain's `cohortSurvivorCount` counts cohorts the GRID selects and returns 125; `rt_survives` -- which also requires a rate -- returns 124, and 124 is what MOVES emits. The binding makes the key set exact, 372 of 372 with none missing and none extra, but it is exact partly by luck: the unrated cohort happens to rank LAST, so truncating the relation drops the right one. A run whose unrated cohort ranked in the middle would emit two wrong keys and this binding would hide it. The rule the chain is missing is the one conventions §67.2 states -- a cohort with no rate is not emitted -- and it belongs in `coh_rank` and in `rspp_isEmitted`, which likewise counts 4 pollutant-processes here where MOVES emits 3, because SO2 passes the road-type test and has no rate at all.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES, which §23 asks for before a dimension is called covered.\n\n* THE RATE TABLE HAS TWO ARMS, AND EVERY EARLIER RUNSPEC USED ONLY ONE. `emissionRate` and `emissionRateByAge` are disjoint in pollutant-process: a pollutant either deteriorates with age or it does not. Tire wear, brake wear and running energy are in the first and every RunSpec in this directory until now read nothing else; THC, CO and NOx running exhaust are in the second, which has an `ageGroupID` key and 1,590,830 rows against the first's 105,449. Before J58 this run returned ZERO for all 375 cells. Conventions §67.1 has the selection that reads one arm or the other and why it is not a sum.\n\n* THE COHORT COUNT IS 124, NOT 125, AND THE RATE TABLE IS WHAT SAYS SO. Every other run in this directory emits 125 cohorts for source type 21. Here the model year 2000 ELECTRICITY cohort has no `emissionRateByAge` row at its age group, so it is not rated, while model years 2001-2020 of the same fuel are rated at zero and are emitted. This is the first run in this port where the RATE table decides the SHAPE of the answer rather than only its values.\n\n* THE NOx HUMIDITY ADJUSTMENT IS LIVE, ON BOTH OF ITS EQUATIONS. `noxhumidityadjust` carries CFR 86 -- linear in bounded specific humidity -- for gasoline and E85, and CFR 1065 -- reciprocal in bounded water mole fraction -- for diesel, and this run exercises both, at 1.0215928 and 1.0095484. No RunSpec here could see either before, because neither energy nor tire wear is NOx.\n\n* THE SURVIVING POLLUTANT-PROCESSES ARE NOT A PREFIX. Eight are selected and three are emitted, at positions 1, 3 and 5. This is the one run in the corpus that would discriminate a renumbering of the output blocks (conventions §60).\n\nTHE FOURTEEN SELECTIONS, ONE BINDING AND THE ORACLE are everything this document carries, as §7.1 asks. Against `runspecs/process-tirewear.esm` it differs in its fourteen `url_template`s, in the one binding, and in nothing else: the same county, month, day, hour, source type and road type, and a different list of pollutant-processes. That is the point -- the dimension under test is the pollutant, and every other selection is held fixed.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-criteria",
      "criteria-pollutants",
      "pending"
    ]
  },
